# Ignore everything without an extension.
*
!*.*
!*/

# Don't ignore makefiles.
!makefile
!Makefile

# Don't ignore license files.
!LICENSE

# Ignore .txt files.
*.txt
!requirements.txt

# Ignore Python cache.
*__pycache__/

# Ignore vscode directory.
*vscode/
*vs/

# Don't ignore the QAcademy Test framework submodule. Git records a submodule as a gitlink, not
# as a directory, so the "!*/" above does not reach it and the extensionless name is caught by
# the "*" on the first line.
!libs/test

# Ignore everything the builds leave behind. This list mirrors ci/clean.sh's targets, which is
# the authority on what this repository generates: anything `make clean` removes is by
# definition something git must not carry. The rules at the top re-include anything with an
# extension, so every generated file type has to be named here explicitly.
build/
temp/
*.o
*.d
*.a
*.pyc

# GHDL work libraries and simulation artifacts. "e~*" is the elaboration file GHDL's mcode
# backend leaves next to the design.
work/
work-obj*.cf
*.ghw
*.vcd
*.fst
e~*

# The Python environment used to redraw the diagrams.
.venv/

# Answers written into an exercise directory while checking that its test suite or testbench
# accepts a correct one.
#
# An exercise directory holds the suite we ship and nothing else: the implementation is the
# reader's to write, and shipping it early is the one thing the course design rules out.
# Solving an exercise locally, to check the material we hand out actually accepts a correct
# answer, leaves that solution sitting in the working tree where a single `git add -A` would
# publish it. These lines make "not pushed" structural rather than a thing to remember.
#
# Published reference implementations live in lectures/*/solutions/, which is deliberately not
# matched here.
#
# The C++ patterns reach one level only, because a gitignore "*" never crosses a "/". So the
# shipped suites under exercises/test/ are untouched by them and need no re-include -- and must
# not be given one, because a "!.../test/**" would also un-ignore the compiled binary that the
# rules at the top of this file are there to hide. The VHDL pattern has to reach one level
# deeper, because a VHDL exercise is a directory rather than a file, so it names the testbench
# back in explicitly.
lectures/*/exercises/*.cpp
lectures/*/exercises/*.hpp
lectures/*/exercises/*/*.vhd
!lectures/*/exercises/*/*_tb.vhd
